\chapter{Atomic poll and heterogeneous readiness}
\label{ch:43}
\index{Atomic poll and heterogeneous readiness}
\index{Channels!poll}

\OnxCodeTerm{poll} presents several eligible channel or captured-stream operations as one choice. A receive arm binds exactly the payload type of its endpoint. EOF has its own outcome. A blocked send must observe backpressure and cannot commit merely because an earlier probe found room. Captured stdout can participate alongside an ordinary channel without converting the ordinary payload to bytes.

\section{Exercise competing arms}
Use one normal typed sender and one captured byte-stream receiver. Make each become ready first in separate tests. Check the bound payload in the selected arm, and exercise a stream that closes before returning data. If a timer is also present, verify the deadline case in a separate run that does not depend on the scheduler's ordering for simultaneous readiness. Runtime registration and withdrawal belong to the atomic wait. A handwritten loop of \OnxCodeTerm{try\_} calls can have different scheduling and selection behavior.

